      "id": "estuary-sensor-v1",
      "prompt": "Field note: The estuary monitoring station reports temperature, salinity, and dissolved oxygen at the start and end of each observation period. Technicians use the same calibrated probe for both readings, rinse it with sample water, and record the tide direction before interpreting a change. In the morning the surface temperature was 14.2 degrees Celsius, salinity was 28.4 practical salinity units, and dissolved oxygen was 8.1 milligrams per liter. By late afternoon the temperature was 17.0 degrees Celsius, salinity was 25.9, and dissolved oxygen was 7.3. Rain fell upstream during the interval, and the tide was ebbing at the second reading. The team does not have a simultaneous deep-water sample or a direct measurement of river discharge. A supervisor asks for a concise interpretation that separates observations from hypotheses. State what changed numerically, give two plausible mechanisms, and say which additional measurements would help distinguish them. Avoid claiming that one cause is established by these two samples alone. Answer in three short paragraphs with no bullet points.\n\nInterpretation:",
