      "id": "textile-catalog-v1",
      "prompt": "Catalog note: A small museum receives a woven wall hanging with no original label. The object has a linen ground, indigo and madder-colored threads, and a narrow band of repaired stitching along one edge. The donor remembers that it hung in a family home before 1960, but cannot identify the maker or the place of manufacture. A handwritten inventory card from the 1980s calls it a nineteenth-century village weaving without citing an examination or acquisition record. The registrar can photograph the front and reverse, measure the weave and thread count, document the repair, and consult conservation staff. Destructive sampling is not approved. The collection database needs a public description that is useful to visitors without turning an unverified family memory or old inventory label into a fact. Draft a cautious entry that names what is directly visible, marks the date and origin as uncertain, and suggests a practical next research step. Keep the tone plain, specific, and respectful of the unknown maker. Do not infer cultural ownership from the colors alone or claim that the repair is original.\n\nPublic description:",
      "continuation": "\nThis woven wall hanging has a linen ground with indigo and madder-colored threads and a repaired edge.",
